    overview:
      "Welcome to French contract law. French law is codified in the Code civil, substantially modernized by a 2016 reform that dropped the old cause requirement and made good faith and specific performance far more central than they are in common-law systems, including a hardship doctrine French law had famously rejected for over a century before finally adopting it. Over the next forty minutes you will work through ten ideas in the order a real French contract dispute actually unfolds. We start at the threshold, how a contract actually forms under the post-2016 framework, who has the capacity to form one, and who can act on another's behalf in forming it. We then turn to the ways a validly formed contract can still unravel through a defect in one party's actual consent, before covering what happens once performance goes wrong: the remedy French law reaches for first, the broader menu of remedies available beyond it, and the doctrine that finally let a French court revise a contract for genuinely unforeseen hardship after more than a century of refusing to. We turn next to the statutory control French law imposes on unfair standard terms, and the clock that eventually runs out on every claim. We close with the principle that has quietly shaped everything covered before it: the general, codified duty of good faith running through negotiation, formation, and performance alike. Keep one comparison running underneath everything you hear: where would a US lawyer instinctively reach for consideration, an exceptional equitable remedy, or an open-ended unconscionability standard, and what does the Code civil actually put in that exact spot instead.",
