        explanation:
          "Now, turn to who is actually capable of forming one of these declarations of consent. Article 1145 of the Code civil states the general rule plainly: every person may contract unless declared incapable by law, and incapacity is treated as the exception requiring specific statutory grounds, not the default. Article 1146 identifies the two principal categories of incapacity, minority, minorité, and judicial protection of a vulnerable adult, majeurs protégés. The 2007 reform of adult protection law, Loi n° 2007-308 du 5 mars 2007, restructured this second category into three distinct regimes of increasing severity: sauvegarde de justice, the lightest and most temporary, leaving the adult's own capacity essentially intact while providing a safety net against specific harmful acts; curatelle, requiring a curator's assistance, co-signature, for significant acts without removing the adult's ability to act at all; and tutelle, full representation, under which the tuteur acts on the protected adult's behalf for most significant legal acts. Article 1148, by contrast, confirms that a capable or incapable person alike may still validly perform ordinary, everyday acts authorized by law or custom, usage, without triggering any capacity inquiry at all. A legal person, a personne morale, has its own, structurally different capacity question, its ability to contract is instead bounded by its corporate purpose, objet social, and its own governing instruments, a topic the Corporate & Compliance lecture in this series covers in its own right.",
