      "Ten ideas, one recurring comparison: where a common-law lawyer would reach for consideration, an exceptional equitable remedy, or an open-ended unconscionability standard, ask what the Code civil actually puts in that exact spot instead. Formation under the post-2016 framework, capacité, and la représentation answer the threshold question of whether a valid contract exists at all, and who was actually authorized to form it on a party's behalf. Les vices du consentement is how an otherwise validly formed contract can still unravel for a genuine defect in one party's actual consent, erreur, dol, or violence, each targeting a different way that consent can fail to be genuine. Exécution forcée en nature and the broader remedy menu under l'exception d'inexécution govern what happens once performance goes wrong, while théorie de l'imprévision addresses the narrower case of a contract that becomes ruinous through nobody's fault at all, a hardship doctrine the Canal de Craponne court would not even have recognized existed. Clauses abusives is the separate, codified control on unfair standard terms, reaching both consumer contracts and non-negotiated business contracts alike, and la prescription extinctive is the clock running underneath every one of these remedies the whole time, with its own absolute twenty-year ceiling regardless of when a claimant actually discovers their right. Bonne foi, finally, is the pervasive principle running underneath everything else in this lecture, from the earliest pre-contractual negotiation through to the final performance of an already-formed contract, and it is worth noticing how often the concepts covered earlier turn out, on closer inspection, to be specific applications of this one closing idea. As you read any French contract dispute from here forward, try running it through all ten ideas in order, and resist the reflex to reach for a common-law doctrine that simply has no real counterpart in this system.",
