    connections:
      "Ten ideas, one recurring comparison: where the US Constitution assumes a single, largely separated system, ask what hybrid, layered, or doubled structure French law actually built instead. The 1958 Constitution's semi-presidential structure, and its own 2000 quinquennat recalibration, sets the basic distribution of power that Article 49.3, now narrowed by the 2008 reform, lets the executive lean on directly, and that cohabitation periodically tests and rebalances entirely. The domaine de la loi and du règlement, softened further by Article 38's own ordonnance mechanism, divides lawmaking power between parliament and the executive, while the dual court system, and the Tribunal des conflits standing between the two, divides dispute resolution between the ordinary judicial courts and the administrative courts hearing claims against the state. The Conseil constitutionnel, its own composition explicitly political rather than judicial, is the institution enforcing constitutional limits, now reachable through the QPC as well as the older a priori route, drawing on the full bloc de constitutionnalité, a structure the 1971 Liberté d'association decision itself first made possible, rather than a single text. État d'urgence, and its more recent état d'urgence sanitaire sibling, shows what happens to this entire framework under genuine crisis conditions, and laïcité, rooted in the 1905 separation statute, illustrates how French constitutional principles can produce meaningfully different real-world outcomes than superficially similar-sounding principles elsewhere. The EU law question, anchored in Article 88-1 itself, finally, adds a further, still-evolving layer on top of the entire domestic framework this lecture has covered. As you read any French constitutional or regulatory question from here forward, try running it through all ten ideas in order, and resist the reflex to assume a single, unified system where French law has actually built a hybrid or a doubled one instead.",
